{{!
    @template theme_dimensions/landing_page

    The course enrolment landing page: the hero, in the site's brand colour, with the course image
    behind it and, beside the title, the grid of up to four institutional badges (below the
    text on narrow viewports); a reading column opening with the presentation
    video's media card when there is one, then the summary and the textarea
    sections, the latter in one panel; and the sticky enrolment card holding the
    fact rows, the enrolment area for the resolved state and, for somebody who
    manages the course without an enrolment, a way into it (landing_page::manage()).

    The hero's inside is theme_dimensions/landing_hero, which also documents its two style attribute
    sinks (the image's url and the workload bar's widths). With herofullwidth the site draws the hero
    full width in the page header (theme_dimensions/landing_header) and this page draws none.

    The summary is cut after its third paragraph by theme_dimensions/landingsummary, which unhides
    the "See more" toggle only when there is more; without JavaScript it is whole and the toggle
    stays hidden.

    The hero's emphasis block follows the scenario: the big date of an online
    event, the place of an in-person offering, the double ticket of a hybrid
    event (the date, then the two ways to take part) and the composition bar of
    a hybrid course (its workload split into online and in-person hours). A
    hybrid event offering two or more methods draws each one as a track, headed
    by the instance's own name.

    A trail's page (the trail model) puts its courses in the hero's glass, one segment per course,
    and draws them in order after the summary, each a numbered card with its cover, category, hours
    and dates; a ghost card, a visitor's for a course the catalogue lists but does not serve to the
    public, carries its number and nothing of the course (landing_page::trail_part()). The covers
    are images, never a style attribute. For a learner enrolled in the trail each card carries its
    state (done, current, late, locked, upcoming: a class and data-state from that fixed vocabulary),
    the hero counts the courses done, and the card's button continues at the next step's activity.
    An account's card also leads to the calendar's Trilhas view with this trail kept
    (trailcalendarurl, landing_page::calendar_url()).

    The page ends with the related courses band (landing_page::related(), local\related\band), after both
    columns: courses sharing competencies with this course, the ones the reader may see, drawn only when
    there is one, by the partial theme_dimensions/related_band. A [relatedcourses] shortcode written in the
    course's summary or sections is drawn here instead of in the text, with its notice for an editor
    (relatednotice, core's warning) above the band.

    The badge URLs go through the theme's own file route and the alt texts are
    plain spellings. The video's html is the markup of whichever enabled media
    player takes the address first (see landing_page::video()), inserted raw
    inside the 16:9 player box.

    The enrolment buttons carry the markup contracts of the plugins' own AMD
    modules, which the page arms before output: the self-enrolment key button
    and the application button carry data-id, data-instance, data-form and
    data-title attributes read by enrol_self/enrol_page and
    enrol_apply/enrol_page (the apply form's action is the no-JavaScript
    transport); the payment button carries the data attributes read by
    core_payment/gateways_modal (action, component, paymentarea, itemid, cost,
    successurl, description). Fallback widget HTML comes from third-party
    enrol plugins' own page hooks and is inserted raw, as core's enrolment
    page does.

    A visitor is asked to log in only where a login could lead somewhere
    (showlogin). Where guest access is the only way in, its button is the
    primary action under the access heading, with a quiet login link for a
    learner already enrolled; with neither, the card carries
    the closed sentence. Guest access is primary for a logged-in reader too
    when nothing else on the card enrols.

    Example context (json):
